==English==
===Etymology===
{{suffix|nation|ist}}, from {{term|nationism}}, {{w|Joshua Fishman}}, 1968.<ref>Fishman, J.A. 1968. Nationality–nationalism and nation–nationism. Fishman, J.A.; Ferguson, C.A.; Das Gupta, J., ed. ''Language problems of developing nations.'' John Wiley & Sons, New York, NY, USA. pp. 39–51.</ref>

====Adjective====
{{en-adj}}

# {{context|sociolinguistics|lang=en}} A language used for the day-to-day functioning of government – official forms, intra-government communication.
# {{misspelling of|nationalist}}

====Usage notes====
Like {{term|nationism}}, '''nationist''' is a technical term used in sociolinguistics, and is ''contrasted'' with {{term|nationalist}}. However, '''nationist''' may also occur as a misspelling of the far more common {{term|nationalist|nation<u>al</u>ist}}.

